<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of London (1839)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of London of 1839 was signed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the great powers. It made the Netherlands recognise Belgian independence, nine years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a>, divided Limburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> between the two countries and guaranteed Belgium's neutrality. The German invasion of neutral Belgium in 1914 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839.jpg" alt="Treaty of London (1839)" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Big Bad Dog · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belgique_1830.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Since 1815 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> had been a reluctant part of the United Kingdom of the Netherlands. In 1830 Belgians broke away and set up an independent Kingdom of Belgium. The largely Catholic population objected to King William I's favouritism towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>, French-speakers disliked his disdain for their language, and the middle classes resented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> monopoly of public office. There was also unemployment and industrial unrest. About 600 volunteers died in the small-scale fighting and are commemorated in the Place des Martyrs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. An international settlement of 1831, the Treaty of the XVIII Articles, was rejected by the Dutch, who invaded in the autumn of 1831, and it took a French army recapturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1832 before an armistice was agreed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed on 19 April 1839 at the end of the London Conference of 1838 to 1839. The powers recognised the southern provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, independent in fact since 1830, as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and the Province of Limburg was split into Belgian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> parts. The Grand Duchy of Luxemburg, in personal union with the Netherlands and a member of the German Confederation, lost two thirds of its territory to Belgium's new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-luxembourg">Province of Luxembourg</a>. The remaining third stayed in personal union with the Netherlands under William I, a settlement confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1867">Treaty of London</a> of 1867 and lasting until the death of William III on 23 November 1890.</p><p>Article VII required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> to remain perpetually neutral, and the five great powers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the United Kingdom, pledged to guarantee its neutrality. The treaty also guaranteed Belgium a right of transit by rail or canal across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> territory to the German Ruhr, the basis of the later Iron Rhine dispute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty became central to European international law. On 31 July 1914 the Belgian king pointed out that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> were legally bound to respect his country's neutrality. When Germany invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in August 1914 after an unanswered ultimatum, Britain declared war on 4 August. The German chancellor Theobald von Bethmann Hollweg said he could not believe the two countries would go to war over a scrap of paper. Christopher Clark notes that the British cabinet had agreed on 29 July that the treaty did not oblige Britain to use force, and that fears of German dominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> weighed heavily in its decision. Few historians now hold that Belgium's fate was the real motive for the declaration of war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of London (1839)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of London</b> of 1839 then divided the country: the larger, French-speaking western part became the Belgian Province of Luxembourg, and the smaller, Luxembourgish-speaking east remained a grand duchy of about 2,600 square kilometres, the territory of the present state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of London</b> of 1839 divided Limburg between Belgium and the Netherlands.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-limburg-1839-1867">Duchy of Limburg (1839-1867)</a></li><li><blockquote>William refused to accept this until the <b>Treaty of London</b> in 1839, which left the Kingdom of the Netherlands in the north.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-kingdom-of-the-netherlands">United Kingdom of the Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
